<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dáil Éireann</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Dáil</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dáil Éireann is the lower house of the Irish parliament. It first met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> on 21 January 1919, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinn-fein">Sinn Féin</a> members elected in the 1918 general election declared Irish independence and formed the revolutionary assembly of the Irish Republic. It approved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-irish-treaty">Anglo-Irish Treaty</a> in January 1922, and it continued as the main chamber of the Irish Free State and of Ireland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dail-eireann.jpg" alt="Dáil Éireann" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Houses of the Oireachtas · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dail_Eireann_logo_1.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name means Assembly of Ireland, a dáil being a meeting, tryst or encounter of any kind. The first legislature in Ireland was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ireland">Parliament of Ireland</a>, which sat from 1297 to 1800 and was abolished by the Act of Union of 1800; members elected for Ireland then sat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-commons-of-the-united-kingdom">House of Commons of the United Kingdom</a> until 1922.</p><p>In the United Kingdom general election of 1918 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinn-fein">Sinn Féin</a> contested the Irish seats on a pledge to establish a constituent assembly as the supreme national authority. It won 73 of the 105 Irish seats, and its members refused to recognise the United Kingdom parliament and convened instead as Dáil Éireann, the unicameral legislature of a unilaterally declared Irish Republic and the first Irish parliament since 1801.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first meeting took place in the Mansion House in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> on 21 January 1919, attended by 27 members. One of its first acts was to ratify a provisional constitution. The body was prohibited in the following September and forced underground, meeting in several locations. Neither the First nor the Second Dáil was recognised by the British government, and internationally the Dáil of the Irish Republic was recognised only by the Russian Soviet Federative Socialist Republic. In 1921 the United Kingdom government set up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-southern-ireland">Parliament of Southern Ireland</a>, which nationalists rejected and boycotted.</p><p>On 6 December 1922, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-irish-treaty">Anglo-Irish Treaty</a> of 1921, the Dáil became the lower house of the bicameral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oireachtas">Oireachtas</a> of the new Irish Free State. The first Dáil under its constitution was styled the 3rd Dáil, and every one since has met in Leinster House. On 29 December 1937 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-ireland">Constitution of Ireland</a> replaced the Free State, and the first Dáil to meet under it was the Ninth Dáil. The present Dáil derives from the 1937 constitution, but a continuity from the First Dáil of 1919 is considered to exist.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Dáil is the lower house and principal chamber of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oireachtas">Oireachtas</a>, which also includes the president and the Seanad Éireann. Its members, known as Teachtaí Dála or TDs, are directly elected by proportional representation using the single transferable vote, and the constitution sets their number between one per 20,000 and one per 30,000 of the population. A Dáil may not continue for longer than five years. It is the dominant branch of the Oireachtas: it nominates the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoiseach">Taoiseach</a> for appointment by the president and approves the budget.</p><p>The chairperson, the Ceann Comhairle, is chosen from among the TDs but must observe strict impartiality and votes only in the event of a tie. Debate is generally in English, though TDs may switch between Irish and English. When a vote is taken, the question is first put in Irish, and members answer Tá or Níl.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dáil Éireann” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its members met as <b>Dáil Éireann</b> in January 1919 and declared independence, and the Irish Republican Army fought the Irish War of Independence against British forces from 1919 to 1921.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After Sinn Fein won most Irish seats in the 1918 general election, its members met in Dublin on 21 January 1919 as <b>Dail Eireann</b>, adopted a Declaration of Independence and created a government, with Eamon de Valera as president from April 1919.</blockquote><a href="/state/irish-republic">Irish Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Sinn Fein won 124 of the 128 seats unopposed but treated the election as one for the second <b>Dail</b> of the Irish Republic, so the Parliament of Southern Ireland met only twice, in June 1921 and January 1922.</blockquote><a href="/state/southern-ireland">Southern Ireland</a></li></ul></section>
